Z||000003||Wannabe||02-02-2001||10:24 PM||||Conspiracy hat on: The entire Fiero situation was a case of GM protecting a proven winner in the American sports car arena: the Corvette. Pontiac tried to get a two seater sports car in 1969, but GM said no way, not competiton for the Corvette. So ten years later someone at Pontiac though they could slip on under the radar. Everything was ok as long as "the other" two seater remained a joke. When it started to work well, and with the prospect of some serious power on the horizon, it was time to pull the plug. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||CozmoPooky||02-03-2001||08:21 AM||||Ya gotta love the conspiracy theory thing...<BR>Just another colorful part of the continuing Fiero saga!<BR>It's great to put the wampus on so many other so-called "fast" cars with a lowly Fiero.  I don't know why anyone would take on a Fiero- nothing to gain if they win (gee, I beat a Fiero) and everything to lose if they lose (I got beat by a mumbleFIEROmumble).  As Khan said in "Star Trek II", "Revenge is a dish best enjoyed cold".<BR>Out,<P>------------------<BR>CozmoPooky<BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>'87 Duke Coupe (4.9 Caddy conversion in progress...)<BR> [img=http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero-go.gif]http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero-go.gif[/img] <BR>'85 2M6 Targa||||reg||10
